[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Router4 is up and routing normally. From multiple hosts at Location C (PC11–PC14), the default gateway 192.168.200.1 responds to ICMP with low latency, disproving the “gateway dead” claim (e.g., PC11→192.168.200.1 0% loss; same from PC12–PC14). Router4’s interfaces are up and addressed: eth0=192.168.200.1/24 (LAN C), eth4=192.168.15.2/24 (to R5), eth5=172.25.1.1/24 (to R3). Its kernel routes include multiple RIP-learned prefixes and a default via 172.25.1.2; ICMP to both upstream next hops succeeds from the LAN (PC11→192.168.15.1 and →172.25.1.2 0% loss). End-to-end intersite reachability also works (PC11→172.16.10.4 0% loss; remote PC4→192.168.200.12 0% loss), confirming bidirectional routing through router4.

Why the outage report? Likely user tests targeted a network not present in routing (e.g., 172.18.51.0/24); pings from Location C to that subnet time out, and router4’s table shows no route for 172.18.51.0/24, so packets are just sent to default and may not reach that site by design. This can be misinterpreted as “no way out” if only that destination is tried.

Localization: No failed subsystems found. Router4’s ICMP replies, interface states, and RIP-learned routes are healthy.

Root cause: False alarm/misinterpretation—router4 never failed; users tested reachability to an off-domain network not advertised to Location C.

Corrective fix: No reboot needed. Inform users router4 is operational. If 172.18.51.0/24 (or other) should be reachable, add appropriate routing (RIP advertisement or static/OSPF/BGP as per design) on the upstream routers; otherwise, advise users to test documented reachable prefixes.=== END ===